# text = When the expedition finally reached the ridge after eleven days of slow progress through snow that was sometimes waist-deep, the leader, a soft-spoken geologist from Tromsø who had spent most of her career studying glaciers that no longer existed, sat down on a flat rock, unwrapped a chocolate bar that she had been saving since the first night, and announced, to nobody in particular and to everybody at once, that this was the most beautiful view she had ever seen, which, given everything she had seen, was saying quite a lot, though several members of the team later admitted that they had been too exhausted to notice anything other than the fact that, for once, the ground was not sloping upward.
